          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Be the first team to collect 101 points or more. One game to 101 is called a 'boam' (tree).",
          gameplay: "Player to dealer's left leads. Must follow suit if able. If an opponent is winning, you must beat their card if possible when following suit. If void, must trump if possible (unless partner is winning).",
          keyRules: [
            'Must follow suit if able. If opponent is winning, must beat their card when following suit or overtrump when void; if partner is winning, no obligation to beat or trump.',
            'Undertrumpping is illegal (cannot play a lower trump than the one winning the trick unless only small trumps left).',
            "Dealer chooses trump (or no trump for double); no bidding. Dealer's partner must not look at cards before trump is chosen.",
            'First team to 101 points wins one boam. Card points: 10=5, A=4, K=3, Q=2, J=1; 9/8/7=0. Team with more points in tricks scores (total minus 30); doubles apply for no trump, Ik ga mee, tie, or all tricks.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Lead a Manille (10) of a non-trump suit early - it will likely 'go round' (laten rondgoan) as no one can beat it without trumping. Save your trumps for when you need to win crucial tricks.",
          intermediate: "Create voids (renoang) by discarding your only card of a suit on a trick your partner is winning. This allows you to trump when that suit is led. Avoid being stuck with an 'blooten aas' (lone Ace) - an Ace without other cards of that suit, which is vulnerable to being trumped.",
          advanced: "Count cards carefully - this is essential in Manillen. Track which Manilles and Aces have been played. If you know all trumps are gone, your plain suit cards become more valuable. Use 'vet'n' (fattening) - playing high value cards on your partner's winning trick to capture those points safely.",
          tips: [
            "The 'Boeketje Vlaanderen' (Little Flemish Bouquet) - having 2 cards of every suit - is the hardest hand for choosing trumps",
            'Remember: 10s are the highest cards, not Aces!',
            'If you have many trumps, consider playing no trump (muulen) for the double score',
            "Pay attention to the 'Ik ga mee' - it often indicates the opponent has a strong hand in the trump suit",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
